A||||11||batboy||Locked my keys inside
Z||000000||batboy||07-01-99||09:00 PM||||Ok, like a dummy I locked my only set of keys inside one of my Fieros. I was able to insert a long wire between the side window and the seal, but the wire was too flexible to move the lock lever. Do I need to locate a heavier wire or does anyone have any better suggestions? The nearest locksmith is 20 miles away, so forget that idea, and yes, when I get my keys out, I'm going to immediately get a spare set made.
Z||000001||theogre||07-01-99||09:25 PM||||That's going to be a real bitch. Those GM locks are "guarded" as I recall. The guard is intended to defeet the "slim jim" aproach to opening the door.<p>There is a set of tools that is available to Wrecker operators, Police, and of course Locksmiths, that includes a tool that reaches up under the window to release the lock by grabbing the rod from the button under the door handle. Even when you know what you are doing this tool is a pain in the ass to use.<p>Unfortunatly I'm not where you are. I've rarely met a lock I can't open on a car. I once made very good money off silly people like you. :)<p>Your best bet is to find some Very ridged 3/16-1/4" rod, bend 2-4 inches at the end 90 degrees, and another 90 bend about 3 feet from the first. This rod is then carfully slipped over the window to hit the lock button. This method is best with 2 people. one to move the rod, one to watch from the far side and give direction. With care this method will do minimal damage to the seal. you may need to bow the rod a bit to reach the lock. Use a THIN wood or plastic wedge to hold the window out. Use EXTREME CAUTION or you will break the window.<p>While I realize this sounds king of bad, the best source of such rod that I've found is discard cemetary wreath stands. (I used to live next to a cemetary that had a pile of these left from people who don't properly tend their family plots.) That is one of the most ridged small diameter rods I've ever seen. To make sharp bends may require some heat on the larger rod that makes the tripod. the leg spreader rod cen be bent with pliers.
Z||000002||batboy||07-01-99||11:31 PM||||Hmmmmm, Ogre, are you a professional car thief or just a grave robber? Haha, just joking. I'll see if I can find a similar rod or something equally suitable. I used to be good at getting into my cars that had the vertical lock knobs on top of the door panel by using a coat hanger, but these newfangled locks are harder to beat.
Z||000003||DJRice||07-02-99||07:16 AM||||Do you have power windows?  If not, you could try rolling down the window with a straightened out hanger with a loop on the end.  I havent tried it on a fiero but I did manage to make it work on a Chevy Lumina for a little old lady in a Wal-Mart Parking lot.<br>Of course, obtaining the right tools is a better idea.
Z||000004||batboy||07-02-99||09:33 AM||||Yes, I have power windows. I also have power door locks, but they don't work. I just now tried the heaviest wire that I could find in my scrap pile, but it was still to flexible. I even tried to hook the keys out of the ignition switch with a wire, but that didn't work. I actually (temporarily) entertained the idea of breaking out a window or prying up the sunroof because I was getting mad. I've cooled off now though. Next, I'm either going to check out a nearby cemetery or drive all the way to town to the hardware store to find a piece of rod. Fortunately, I have extra keys to my other cars.
Z||000005||batboy||07-02-99||12:57 PM||||Success! I bought a 3 foot length of 3/16 inch diameter steel rod. I bent a 45 degree angle on the last 2 inches and put a bow into the rod along the entire length. Then, I put a hunk of good ol' duct tape onto the outer trim to keep from marking it up with the rod. I carefully pried the upper rear corner of the window back enough to slip a wedge of wood in. Next, I clamped a small pair of vice-grips onto the other end of the rod to use as a handle. Worked real slick. Ogre, thank you for the suggestions. Now, no locked Fiero is safe from me. ***sinister laughter***
Z||000006||Racer 1||07-02-99||03:24 PM||||break your window with a brick.then call the police and say someone broke into your car so insurance can cover it?LOL
Z||000007||batboy||07-02-99||06:32 PM||||Racer1, that thought did briefly cross my mind, but I have $250 deductible, so I'd still have to pay for the window out of my own pocket. Besides, the deputy probably wouldn't buy my story since we rarely have any crime around here. Doesn't matter now anyway, I got it unlocked and there was no damage. I'm a happy camper now.
Z||000008||theogre||07-02-99||09:22 PM||||Hmmmmmm....... A good game of wake the dead would be fun. :)
Z||000009||peabody||07-03-99||09:22 AM||||If you're not in a hurry, your local GM dealer can get the key code if you supply the <br>VIN. From the key code, they will cut you a fresh key. Perhaps that would be a better way to go to get that second set you promised to have made:) A copy from your current set is likely worn and might not work as smoothly as one cut to new spec.
Z||000010||batboy||07-03-99||12:00 PM||||Peabody, I didn't know that, thanks for the tip!
Z||000011||Gokart||07-04-99||03:15 AM||||Wanted to add for everyone that I locked myself out years ago when I had a coupe. I had left the sunroof open and was able to reach my hand in and pinch the buttons that pop it open and then crawled through the roof!! 
